blind giant sits in the doorway with his hands out.\n\nThe flock is all he can still count, and he counts it by touch.\n\nOdysseus has tied the sheep in threes, with a man hanging under each middle one.\n\nHe saves the best ram for himself, and clings to its belly.\n\nThe giant feels along their backs as they pass, but never their bellies.\n\nEvery sheep is a risk. If one man slips, or one animal bleats, the plan ends in the dark, with a very large hand.\n\nLast of all comes the ram that carries Odysseus.\n\nAnd the giant speaks to it tenderly: \"My good ram\u2026 you lag last of all.\n\nIs it because you know your master has lost his eye?\"\n\nIt is the gentlest moment the giant has in the poem, and it is aimed at a ram.\n\nThen he lets it go.\n\nHe does not know that he has just let his whole victory walk out of the door.\n\nOutside, the men drop from the sheep and drive the whole flock down to the ship."}